The crystalline structure of the synthesized pure ferrites and CNTs covered with ferrites was determined using a D8 Advance diffractometer (Bruker AXS, Karlsruhe, Germany) with a Cu–K α  cathode (λ = 1.54 Å) operating at a 40 kV voltage and a current of 40 mA. Angle X-ray scattering curves were recorded in a Bragg–Brentano geometry, in the diffraction angle (2θ) range: 2–50°, with a step of 0.02° using a LYNXEYE XE-T detector. The size of the crystallite was calculated using the Rietveld refinement, in the TOPAS 6 program, based on the Williamson-Hall theory [ 53 ,  54 ]. The pseudo-Voigt function was used in the description of diffraction line profiles during refinement using the Rietveld method. The R wp  (weighted-pattern factor) and GOF (goodness-of-fit) parameters were used as numerical criteria of the quality of the fit of the calculated data to the experimental diffraction data [ 55 ]. The dislocation density (δ) was calculated using following equation: 1 δ = 1 / D 2 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
