The polymer composites have not shown a noticeable trend in  R met  behavior changes due to differences in manganese ion content in SFs. The results demonstrate that absorption by polymer composites depends on impedance and thickness matching. An EM wave incident on an absorbing material coated with metal on its backside will partially propagate through the absorber, while the other part will be reflected from the first interface due to impedance differences between the absorbing material and free space (air). The propagated EM waves that have reached the interface between the absorber and the metal background will be reflected from the second interface. Reflections from the first and second interfaces interfere when the thickness of the absorber ( t m ) satisfies the quarter-wave compensation condition ( t m  ∼ λ/4) according to the equation [ 94 ,  95 ]: 5 t m = nc 4 f m ε r μ r n = 1 , 3 , 5 … \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
